;; kotoba/pure route response for a REAL cloud-itonami-app route -- the
;; RFC 9728 protected-resource metadata document served at
;; GET /.well-known/oauth-protected-resource/mcp -- on THIS standalone
;; http-service.mjs demo host, not a live cloud-itonami-app process.
;;
;; ADR-2608760000 addendum 2. Sibling of
;; examples/cloud-itonami-health-route.kotoba (addendum 1), and the same
;; kind of claim: the JSON SHAPE is real, the host is not.
;;
;; ## What is real vs. representative here
;;
;; The shape comes from `cloud.itonami.app.oauth-resource/metadata*`
;; (src/cloud/itonami/app/oauth_resource.clj, read 2026-08-29), which
;; builds exactly five keys: `resource`, `resource_name`,
;; `bearer_methods_supported`, `scopes_supported`, `authorization_servers`.
;;
;; THREE of those five are fixed constants in that file and are copied here
;; VERBATIM, so they are real, not representative:
;;   - `resource_name`             "Itonami Cloud MCP"
;;   - `bearer_methods_supported`  ["header"]
;;   - `scopes_supported`          the `scopes` def: mcp:tools,
;;                                 tenant:connect, repository:read,
;;                                 repository:write
;;
;; TWO are derived from a live deployment's configuration and CANNOT be
;; honestly reproduced by a bare demo process:
;;   - `resource`               `(resource-url configuration)` -- the
;;                              deployment's own MCP resource URL.
;;   - `authorization_servers`  `(authorization-servers configuration)`.
;;
;; The second one deserves emphasis, because it makes this route a WEAKER
;; standalone reproduction than /health was: `metadata*` THROWS
;; (`:oauth-resource/no-authorization-server`) when `servers` is empty. A
;; real cloud-itonami-app with no configured authorization server does not
;; serve a degraded document -- it refuses to serve this route at all. This
;; host has no configuration and therefore no authorization server, so what
;; it serves here is a shape demonstration; a real deployment in the same
;; state would 500, not 200. Both fields say `representative-` literally
;; rather than carrying a plausible-looking invented URL.
;;
;; ## What is NOT proven by wiring this route
;;
;; The admission DECISION (is this GET /.well-known/oauth-protected-
;; resource/mcp) on cloud-itonami-app's real server is
;; `oauth_resource_core.kotoba`'s `oauth-resource-route?`, called through
;; `cloud.itonami.app.kotoba-oracle` on the JVM/KIR interpreter. THIS host's
;; admission decision is amu's own shared `runtime/http/route-decide.kotoba`
;; (unmodified, table-lookup based) -- the same core every other route here
;; uses. The two agree, but this wiring does not exercise
;; `oauth_resource_core.kotoba` at all. That separate, more precise claim is
;; proven by scripts/cloud-itonami-oauth-resource-parity.cljs.
(ns examples.cloud-itonami-oauth-resource-route
  (:export [oauth-protected-resource]))

(defn oauth-protected-resource
  []
  :string
  (string-concat "{\"resource\":\"representative-no-resource-url-configured-standalone-amu-http-service-demo\","
    (string-concat "\"resource_name\":\"Itonami Cloud MCP\","
      (string-concat "\"bearer_methods_supported\":[\"header\"],"
        (string-concat "\"scopes_supported\":[\"mcp:tools\",\"tenant:connect\",\"repository:read\",\"repository:write\"],"
          "\"authorization_servers\":[\"representative-no-authorization-server-configured-on-this-host\"]}")))))
